   "oldText": "## Task task-28d3df76 — connect/extend aligned marker strokes (rule unresolved)\nThis task appeared with different palette and dimensions across three demos (marker colors 5, 8, 9; backgrounds 6, 1, 0) and a test (marker 2 on background 7). The demos introduce color 3 in stretches/regions while retaining marker cells. The exact transformation was not solved: all 8 test submissions were incorrect. Do **not** rely on the older claim that simple filling between consecutive aligned marker cells was confirmed; that claim was wrong without further evidence.\n",
   "newText": "## Task task-28d3df76 — join aligned marker strokes (solved)\n**Confirmed rule:** Treat the non-background cells as marker points. For every row and every column, connect consecutive marker cells (consecutive in the ordered list of markers on that line) with color 3 in all intervening background cells; retain all original marker cells and leave unrelated background unchanged. Equivalently, draw horizontal and vertical straight strokes between adjacent aligned markers, without extending past endpoints. The palette/dimensions vary; color 3 is the stroke color.\n\n**Direct evidence from solved instance (10x25; background 0, markers 6):** The submitted output was confirmed CORRECT. It is reproduced in the solved conversation. Examples: row 1 (0-indexed) markers c4,c12,c18,c23 cause spans between each successive pair; row 2 markers c3,c9,c10,c11,c16,c24 cause fills c4-8, c12-15, c17-23; vertically aligned markers also get connected, e.g. column c3 between r2 and r4. Intervening marker cells are preserved, and no stroke extends beyond the first/last marker in a line.\n\nThe task previously seemed unresolved because older exploratory notes concerned other grids with the same task label and recorded unsuccessful/ambiguous hypotheses. Those notes are superseded by this confirmed instance: use the straightforward horizontal+vertical consecutive-aligned-pair rule, rather than convex hull, bounding-box, proximity, or unrestricted region filling. Earlier demos are consistent with this rule. For future recurrences, extract marker locations and fill all spans between successive markers in each row and column, preserving marker color."
